WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.041
Hi, and welcome.

00:00:01.041 --> 00:00:05.008
The art club is planning a campaign for a school food drive.

00:00:05.008 --> 00:00:12.031
They could ask an AI tool for poster ideas, but a fast pile of suggestions is not the same as a clear plan.

00:00:12.031 --> 00:00:20.226
As you watch, keep one question in mind: Start with a purpose, compare ideas against it, and let people shape the final work.

00:00:20.226 --> 00:00:23.152
You do not need to memorize a list of terms.

00:00:23.152 --> 00:00:30.696
Try to notice what the people in the story know, what they still need to check, and what choice they can make next.

00:00:31.246 --> 00:00:36.503
A creative brief is like agreeing on the destination before a group starts building.

00:00:36.503 --> 00:00:42.198
It helps people decide what the work should do, who it is for, and what boundaries matter.

00:00:42.198 --> 00:00:45.702
This comparison is a way to think, not a perfect match.

00:00:45.702 --> 00:00:52.524
Real AI systems can work in different ways, so it helps to ask what this particular tool was designed to do.

00:00:52.524 --> 00:00:55.965
Write a brief with audience, purpose, and constraints.

00:00:55.965 --> 00:00:59.470
What detail in the story makes that goal easier to see?

00:01:00.020 --> 00:01:04.355
The club learns that some students cannot read the small print easily.

00:01:04.355 --> 00:01:09.990
Accessibility is not a final decoration; it changes the design choices from the beginning.

00:01:09.990 --> 00:01:11.228
Pause for a moment.

00:01:11.228 --> 00:01:14.758
What changed between the first situation and the second?

00:01:14.758 --> 00:01:18.659
If the result changed too, that is a clue worth investigating.

00:01:18.659 --> 00:01:20.765
It is not proof of the cause yet.

00:01:20.765 --> 00:01:26.276
Think of one more example that could help separate a genuine pattern from a coincidence.

00:01:26.826 --> 00:01:30.510
One draft is funny but hard to read from across the hallway.

00:01:30.510 --> 00:01:33.089
Another is clear but leaves out the date.

00:01:33.089 --> 00:01:38.983
Comparing them against the goal gives the club a reason to revise instead of picking at random.

00:01:38.983 --> 00:01:42.299
A useful explanation connects a detail to a decision.

00:01:42.299 --> 00:01:48.132
Try finishing this sentence in your own words: I would pay attention to this detail because...

00:01:48.132 --> 00:01:50.650
Compare ideas against the same criteria.

00:01:50.650 --> 00:01:54.334
You can use a different example from the one in the lesson.

00:01:54.334 --> 00:01:58.386
The goal is to explain the reasoning, not to repeat a definition.

00:01:58.936 --> 00:02:02.852
Show a draft to someone who was not in the planning conversation.

00:02:02.852 --> 00:02:09.298
Ask what they think the poster wants them to do, then check whether the details are readable and accurate.

00:02:09.298 --> 00:02:13.455
Before looking at any result, say what you expect to happen and why.

00:02:13.455 --> 00:02:16.889
Then compare your prediction with what actually happens.

00:02:16.889 --> 00:02:22.552
If they do not match, keep the surprising result; it may be the most useful part of the test.

00:02:23.102 --> 00:02:26.783
Suppose every generated design uses the same familiar image.

00:02:26.783 --> 00:02:32.366
What other visual direction could better fit your school and still make the message clear?

00:02:32.366 --> 00:02:36.231
It is tempting to judge a tool by its most impressive example.

00:02:36.231 --> 00:02:42.980
A more careful check also asks who was not represented, what information is missing, and what could go wrong.

00:02:42.980 --> 00:02:45.863
Plan a human revision and accessibility check.

00:02:45.863 --> 00:02:50.342
A person should be able to question a result and choose what to do next.

00:02:50.892 --> 00:03:01.296
For a food-drive poster, the team might keep the warm colors from one suggestion, rewrite the headline in its own voice, and verify the collection date with the organizer.

00:03:01.296 --> 00:03:03.243
Work through it in three moves.

00:03:03.243 --> 00:03:05.981
First, say what job the tool is meant to do.

00:03:05.981 --> 00:03:11.152
Next, point to the evidence you have, rather than a guess about how the tool thinks.

00:03:11.152 --> 00:03:14.073
Finally, decide what still needs a human check.

00:03:14.073 --> 00:03:18.636
There may be more than one reasonable answer if you explain your evidence.

00:03:19.186 --> 00:03:24.228
Now open the practice activity, Creative director's desk, when you are ready.

00:03:24.228 --> 00:03:28.615
During the creative activity, compare options against the purpose.

00:03:28.615 --> 00:03:33.918
Give each revision a reason, such as readability, audience, accuracy, or access.

00:03:33.918 --> 00:03:36.603
Take a first try before asking for help.

00:03:36.603 --> 00:03:40.793
If feedback changes your mind, name the detail that changed it.

00:03:40.793 --> 00:03:44.722
If you keep your answer, explain what evidence supports it.

00:03:45.272 --> 00:03:48.666
Your lesson project is called Create a campaign concept.

00:03:48.666 --> 00:03:54.909
For your project, show how a person will make the final choices and credit any AI assistance honestly.

00:03:54.909 --> 00:03:58.545
The tool can offer possibilities; the team owns the result.

00:03:58.545 --> 00:04:03.151
Before you finish, imagine someone else using your idea for the first time.

00:04:03.151 --> 00:04:04.970
What would they need to know?

00:04:04.970 --> 00:04:08.243
What could they do if the tool did not work for them?

00:04:08.243 --> 00:04:11.576
Those questions can make a promising idea more useful.

00:04:12.126 --> 00:04:17.209
Pick one word from today's lesson and explain it using the story you just heard.

00:04:17.209 --> 00:04:19.941
Avoid copying the wording from the screen.

00:04:19.941 --> 00:04:23.436
You might begin, In this situation, this word means...

00:04:23.436 --> 00:04:27.248
If your explanation is hard to follow, add a small example.

00:04:27.248 --> 00:04:33.030
Clear language is a good way to notice what you understand and what you want to ask about.

00:04:33.580 --> 00:04:40.541
Here is one idea to take with you: Start with a purpose, compare ideas against it, and let people shape the final work.

00:04:40.541 --> 00:04:45.396
Think of a different class or school situation where the same question could help.

00:04:45.396 --> 00:04:49.783
You can write down one thing you would check and one person you could ask.

00:04:49.783 --> 00:04:54.580
That is enough for a useful next step; you do not have to solve every part today.

00:04:55.130 --> 00:04:57.577
Thanks for thinking this through with me.

00:04:57.577 --> 00:05:03.547
Finish the activity and project on the lesson page, then try the separate quiz when you feel ready.

00:05:03.547 --> 00:05:09.456
If a question feels difficult, return to the example and explain your reasoning in your own words.

00:05:09.456 --> 00:05:14.530
Start with a purpose, compare ideas against it, and let people shape the final work.

00:05:14.530 --> 00:05:16.858
The goal is not to trust every answer.

00:05:16.858 --> 00:05:20.738
It is to know what to check and how to make a thoughtful choice.
